---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import HiveSplitDemo from "../components/HiveSplitDemo.astro";
import TrialChart from "../components/TrialChart.astro";
import { LATEST_VERSION } from "../data/releases";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const guides = (await getCollection("guides")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Base title="Bunpō (分蜂) — Balance CI test suites by timing, verify every file once">
	<section class="hero">
		<div>
			<span class="eyebrow">分蜂 · the bees dividing their hive</span>
			<h1>Balance CI test suites by timing and verify every assignment.</h1>
			<p class="lead">
				Split tests by name and one machine gets the slow files while the others sit idle.
				Bunpō remembers how long each file took, deals them out longest first, and fails the
				build if a stale cache leaves a file unassigned or duplicated. It is one static binary
				with no server. Timings live in your own CI cache.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a href="/install" class="btn">Install {LATEST_VERSION}</a>
				<a href="/guides" class="btn btn-ghost">Read a runner guide</a>
			</div>
		</div>
		<figure class="hero-art">
			<picture>
				<source srcset="/brand/mitsu.webp" type="image/webp" />
				<img
					src="/brand/mitsu.png"
					alt="Mitsu, a round honey-brown and cream bee with pale mint wings and a honeycomb satchel, holding out a hexagonal cell in watercolor and ink"
					width="264"
					height="264"
				/>
			</picture>
			<figcaption class="hero-caption">Mitsu · cells of equal weight</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</section>

	<dl class="facts" aria-label="Measured gains and facts">
		<div class="fact">
			<dt>Postgres suite (4 machines)</dt>
			<dd class="gain-positive">25m 30s → 6m 16s (4.0x)</dd>
		</div>
		<div class="fact">
			<dt>Playwright CI (2 runners)</dt>
			<dd class="gain-positive">19m 47s → 9m 58s (-49.6%)</dd>
		</div>
		<div class="fact">
			<dt>binary</dt>
			<dd>3.2 MiB, Go stdlib only</dd>
		</div>
		<div class="fact">
			<dt>hosting</dt>
			<dd>None, uses your CI cache</dd>
		</div>
	</dl>

	<section class="band" id="benchmarks">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Before &amp; after</strong>
				<span>Measured time saved</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Cut 19 minutes off a Postgres suite, and 2 minutes off a count-based split.</h2>
				<p>
					Measured machine times from two production repositories (local 4-CPU trials and a
					live GitHub Actions Playwright run on <code>v0.0.5</code>) alongside the declared
					40-file benchmark. Switch suites to compare before and after on each machine.
				</p>
				<TrialChart />
				<p class="small" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
					<a href="/benchmarks">Read all benchmark tables, tight splits, and indivisible-file limits →</a>
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="how-it-works">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>How it works</strong>
				<span>Split, record, verify, merge</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Deal the slowest files first, then verify every machine saw the same split.</h2>
				<p>
					Take six files that run in 9, 7, 6, 5, 4, and 3 minutes across two machines.
					Splitting alphabetically stacks the three heaviest files onto machine 0 (22 minutes)
					while machine 1 finishes in 12. Bunpō deals longest first to whichever machine has
					the smallest load: 9 + 5 + 3 and 7 + 6 + 4, 17 minutes each, cutting 5 minutes off
					the build. Each machine writes its share and a SHA-256 digest of its inputs into a
					manifest, and <code>bunpo verify</code> checks every manifest at the end so a stale
					cache cannot drop or double-run a file.
				</p>
				<HiveSplitDemo />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="workflow">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>In your pipeline</strong>
				<span>Four plain commands</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Standard output carries file lists. Messages go to standard error.</h2>
				<ol class="steps-list">
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">split</span>
						<div>
							<strong><code>bunpo split --index N --total M</code></strong> reads the test
							list from standard input, prints machine N's share, and writes{" "}
							<code>.bunpo/manifest-N.json</code> only after the full list is written.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">run</span>
						<div>
							<strong>Your test runner</strong> executes those exact files and writes a
							JUnit XML report or a two-column timing file. Empty shards skip the runner
							so it cannot run the whole suite by accident.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">record</span>
						<div>
							<strong><code>bunpo record --index N report.xml</code></strong> sums exact
							case durations per file, checks the report against the manifest, and refuses
							unassigned files. Files with failures stay seen without recording a bad time.
						</div>
					</li>
					<li>
						<span class="step-cmd">verify</span>
						<div>
							<strong><code>bunpo verify</code> and <code>bunpo merge</code></strong> run
							in a final job. <code>verify</code> proves every file was assigned once from
							the same inputs; <code>merge</code> updates the rolling 5-run median in{" "}
							<code>timings.json</code> and drops files unseen for 30 runs.
						</div>
					</li>
				</ol>
				<p class="small muted" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
					<code>verify</code> checks assignments, not execution. Your workflow still checks
					runner exit statuses and expected reports; every guide below wires up both.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="demo">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>In the terminal</strong>
				<span>Exit 0, 1, or 2</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>See split, record, merge, and verify catching a stale timing cache.</h2>
				<p>
					In GitHub Actions, warnings and failures become workflow annotations automatically.
					Exit status is <code>0</code> on success, <code>1</code> when a check fails, and{" "}
					<code>2</code> on bad input or flags.
				</p>
				<div class="demo-frame">
					<img
						src="/demo.gif"
						alt="Terminal recording of bunpo split, record, merge, and verify catching a stale timing cache on machine 2"
						width="1160"
						height="640"
						loading="lazy"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="band" id="guides">
		<div class="band-head">
			<div class="band-label">
				<strong>Guides</strong>
				<span>Tested on real runners</span>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h2>Complete GitHub Actions workflows for five runners.</h2>
				<p>
					Each guide is tested against the real runner with decoy files and glob characters,
					because runners like Jest, Vitest, and Playwright treat plain file arguments as
					patterns unless you lock them down.
				</p>
				<ul class="runner-list">
					{
						guides.map((g) => (
							<li>
								<a href={`/guides/${g.id}`} class="runner-row">
									<span class="runner-name">{g.data.title}</span>
									<span class="runner-detail">
										{g.data.reportFormat} · {g.data.selection}
									</span>
									<span class="runner-arrow">guide →</span>
								</a>
							</li>
						))
					}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
